  "theory": {
    "intro": "When one side of a convex polygon is extended, it forms an exterior angle with the next side. Walking once around any convex polygon turns you through one full revolution, so the exterior angles always add to $360^{\\circ}$, no matter how many sides there are.",
    "facts": [
      "The exterior angles of any convex polygon add to $360^{\\circ}$.",
      "An interior angle and its exterior angle are supplementary: they add to $180^{\\circ}$.",
      "To find a missing exterior angle, subtract the known exterior angles from $360^{\\circ}$.",
      "This holds for every convex polygon regardless of the number of sides — a triangle, a quadrilateral, a pentagon, and so on.",
      "If a polygon has one odd exterior angle and the rest equal, form an equation such as $A+B(n-1)=360$ to find the number of sides $n$."
    ],
    "steps": [
      "Add the known exterior angles.",
      "Set the total equal to $360^{\\circ}$.",
      "Solve for the unknown."
    ]
  },
